{
  "_about": [
    "Word lists a pattern rule can name instead of spelling them out: {{us-state}} is every name below,",
    "{{us-state-code}} every code. One list, so the rule that finds a state, the rule that finds the city",
    "before one and the rule that finds the ZIP after one cannot come to disagree about what a state is.",
    "Spanish names are here because the transcripts are: a caller in Phoenix says Nuevo México.",
    "Puerto Rico and the District of Columbia are in, because Safe Harbor's 'state' is the unit above a",
    "city and the census table for postal codes counts them.",
    "The word lists under \"words\" are named the same way: {{street-suffix-us}} is that list. Each is taken",
    "from a published standard, cited beside it, and not from any transcript."
  ],
  "_words": {
    "sentence-opener": "Words that open a sentence and are capitalised for that reason alone. A rule that reads a capitalised word as a name or a place skips them: 'Yes, Arizona' names no city.",
    "directional-us": "USPS Publication 28, section 233: the directionals, spelled out and abbreviated.",
    "street-suffix-us": "USPS Publication 28, Appendix C1: the primary street suffix names and their standard abbreviations. Used only after a house number, where 'Walk' and 'Park' are streets.",
    "thoroughfare-us": "The suffixes of Appendix C1 that name a road and nothing else in ordinary English, spelled out. Used without a house number, where 'Court', 'Park', 'Way' and every abbreviation ('St' is also Saint, 'Dr' also Doctor) would take an institution or a person for a street.",
    "unit-us": "USPS Publication 28, Appendix C2: secondary unit designators, spelled out and abbreviated.",
    "vialidad-mx": "INEGI, Norma Técnica sobre Domicilios Geográficos (DOF 12 nov 2010): the types of road, with their usual abbreviations."
